course: biology
lesson: 83
title: The elephant house: herds, feet, food and the mouse myth
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: An elephant herd is a family of mothers, daughters, aunts and babies, and at the front walks the grandmother, the matriarch, who remembers where the water is and which dangers to avoid.
explain: In the elephant house the floor is deep, warm sand instead of concrete, because an animal as heavy as several cars gets sore feet and joints on hard ground, and keepers trim its toenails with a big rasp.
explain: Stand still and you may feel a deep rumble more than hear it: elephants talk with sounds so low that some are below what our ears can catch, and they travel kilometres.
explain: And the mouse? In cartoons an elephant shrieks and jumps on a stool when a mouse runs past, but real elephants are not afraid of mice; they may just step aside from anything that moves suddenly by their feet, as you would.
fact: Elephant herds are led by the oldest female, the matriarch; grown-up males leave as teenagers and live alone or in groups of young bulls.
fact: Elephants are not afraid of mice; they cannot see well right by their feet, so a sudden movement there can startle them, but terror is a cartoon joke.
fact: Modern elephant houses have deep sand and heated floors, because years on hard concrete hurt elephants' feet and joints.
fact: An elephant walks on its tiptoes with a big fatty pad behind the toes, and zoo elephants walk less than wild ones, so their nails do not wear down and they are trained to lift their feet so keepers can trim them.
fact: Hay nets hung high make elephants stretch and pick as they would from trees; wild elephants feed for up to 16 hours a day.
fact: An adult elephant can eat more than 100 kilograms of food a day and digests only about half of it, so its dung is full of plant fibre.
fact: Many zoos turn elephant dung into compost for gardens, and in some countries it is even made into paper.
fact: Elephants are pregnant for about 22 months, longer than any other land animal, and a newborn weighs about 100 kilograms and often walks under its mother's belly for shade and safety.
fact: An elephant's ears help it hear and lose heat, but cannot lift it; African elephants have huge ears and two «fingers» at the trunk tip; Asian elephants have smaller ears, a rounder back and one «finger».
fact: Scientists now count two kinds of African elephant, the savanna elephant and the smaller forest elephant, as well as the Asian elephant.
fact: Elephants talk with very low rumbles that travel far, and they may feel them through their feet as well as hear them.
fact: Many zoos have stopped elephant shows such as painting, because training for tricks was sometimes harsh, and now train elephants only for health care, using rewards.
fact: Elephants swim well, using the trunk held above the water like a snorkel.
exercise: Who leads an elephant herd? | The oldest female, called the matriarch | The biggest bull with the longest tusks | The youngest baby | The keeper chooses a leader
exercise: Why does the family follow the matriarch? | She remembers where food and water are and what to avoid | She is always the fastest runner in the herd, so nobody can keep up with her | She has the loudest trumpet | She is the only one who can see
exercise: What happens to male elephants when they grow up? | They leave the herd and live alone or with young bulls | They become the leaders of the herd and its oldest females | They stay with their mothers for life | They are sent to other zoos at birth
exercise: Are elephants really scared of mice? | No, but a sudden movement by their feet can startle them | Yes, mice are their greatest enemy in the wild and in zoos | Yes, mice crawl up their trunks | No, elephants eat mice
exercise: Why do modern elephant houses have deep sand floors? | Hard concrete hurts heavy elephants' feet and joints | Sand is cheaper and much quicker to clean than concrete | Elephants eat the sand for its minerals | Sand stops elephants from swimming
exercise: Why must zoo elephants have their toenails trimmed? | They walk less than wild elephants, so the nails do not wear | Their nails are poisonous and could hurt a keeper badly | Visitors do not like long nails | Elephants scratch the keepers with them if they are left to grow too long
exercise: How does an elephant really stand? | On its tiptoes, with a fatty pad behind the toes | Flat on its heels, just like a person standing still | On its knees most of the time | On hooves like a horse
exercise: Why is hay hung high in a net? | So elephants stretch and pick, as from trees | To keep it safe from the mice that run about on the floor | So visitors cannot steal it | Because elephants cannot bend their necks
exercise: How long can wild elephants spend feeding each day? | Up to about 16 hours | About 1 hour each morning | About 5 minutes in all | They eat only once a week
exercise: Why is elephant dung good for compost? | Elephants digest only about half their food, so it is full of fibre | It is full of meat scraps | It is mostly sand from the floor | It contains no plant matter at all, only water and salt from their bodies
exercise: How long is an elephant pregnant? | About 22 months | About 9 months | About 2 months | About 5 years
exercise: Why does a baby elephant walk under its mother's belly? | For shade and safety | To drink the rainwater that drips off her | Because it cannot see where to go | To keep its trunk dry
exercise: How can you tell an African elephant from an Asian one? | The African has much bigger ears and two trunk «fingers» | The African has a pink trunk | The Asian elephant has four tusks, two on each side of the trunk | The Asian elephant has a striped back
exercise: How many kinds of elephant do scientists now count? | Three: savanna, forest and Asian | One, all elephants are the same kind | Two: the big ones and the small ones | Ten, one for each African country
exercise: How do elephants keep in touch kilometres apart? | With very low rumbles that travel far | By flashing their big ears in the sunshine | By leaving notes of dung along the paths | They cannot keep in touch so far apart
exercise: Why have many zoos stopped elephant painting shows? | Training for tricks was sometimes harsh, so now it is for health care | Elephants used up far too much paint, and the paint made them ill | Visitors did not like the paintings | Elephants are colour blind and cannot paint
exercise: How does an elephant breathe while swimming? | It holds its trunk up like a snorkel | It holds its breath for a whole day | It breathes through its ears | It cannot swim at all
test: In a cartoon an elephant screams and climbs on a chair when a mouse runs in. What would you fix? | A real elephant may step aside, but is not afraid of mice | Right, mice are an elephant's worst fear, everyone knows that | Right, elephants faint when they see mice | Elephants chase and eat mice instead
test: 4 elephants in the house. how many trunks and how many ears altogether | 4 trunks and 8 ears | 8 trunks and 4 ears | 4 trunks and 4 ears | 16 trunks and 8 ears
test: «just put 'em on concrete, way easier to hose down» What would happen? | Their feet and joints would get sore over the years | Nothing, elephants' feet are as hard as iron and never hurt | They would walk faster and be happier | Their toenails would fall off at once
test: Dumbo flies by flapping his huge ears. What do real elephant ears do? | Help them hear and lose heat, but cannot lift them | Let African elephants glide short distances from hill to hill | Only help them swim | Nothing, the ears are just for show
test: «why's the grandma in charge, the big bull's way stronger» What is right? | She remembers where water and food are and what is dangerous | The bull is too lazy to lead, so he lets the grandma do all the work | Grandmothers are the fastest runners | The keepers make her the leader
test: What would happen to elephant numbers if many adults were killed? | They would recover very slowly, as each baby takes years | They would recover in a single season, as elephants breed fast | Nothing, elephants have dozens of babies a year | The babies would grow up faster to fill the gap
test: A herd has 3 mothers, 2 aunts and 4 calves. How many legs are there in the herd? | 36 | 32 | 9 | 40
test: «elephant poo's just rubbish, yeah?» What does the zoo do with it? | Turns it into compost for gardens | Tips it into the sea in big lorries | Gives it back to the elephants to eat | Burns it to heat the elephant house
test: You feel a deep shaking through the floor but hear almost nothing. What could it be? | An elephant rumbling too low for us to hear well | The elephants snoring in their sleep | An earthquake caused by elephant footsteps on the hard ground | A tiger roaring next door
test: Which is true about the elephant you see swimming with only the tip of its trunk out? | It breathes through the trunk like a snorkel | It is drowning and needs help | It is drinking the whole pool | It is smelling the water for fish to eat
test: An elephant with small ears and a rounded back is in front of you. Which kind is it most likely? | An Asian elephant | An African savanna elephant | An African forest elephant with giant ears | A small woolly mammoth
exam: Identify the principal function of the matriarch within an elephant herd. | She retains knowledge of food, water and dangers | She hunts on behalf of the herd | She defends the herd against bulls by fighting them | She bears all of the herd's offspring
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the reaction of elephants to mice. | Elephants may be startled by sudden movement near their feet | Elephants experience acute terror in the presence of mice | Elephants regularly prey upon mice | Mice are known to enter the trunk and suffocate elephants
exam: Determine the principal reason for which modern elephant houses are furnished with deep sand floors. | Standing upon concrete injures the feet and joints of so heavy an animal | Sand is consumed by elephants as a dietary supplement | Sand prevents elephants from swimming | Concrete is prohibited by law in all zoological collections of the world
exam: It is given that the gestation of the elephant is the longest of any land animal. State its approximate duration. | Twenty-two months | Nine months | Two months | Five years
